id: rust.atomics
kind: rust
title: Atomics
aliases: atomic, AtomicU64, fetch_add, lock free counters
tags: concurrency, performance
questions:
  - how do you share a counter between threads without a lock?
invariant: An atomic update is indivisible and ordered by a memory ordering you must choose.
answer_30s: Atomics update single values without a lock: Relaxed for counters and gauges, Acquire/Release when the atomic publishes other data.
answer_2m: |
  Start from the honest default: Relaxed is correct for a metric or an id
  generator where the only requirement is no lost updates. Acquire/Release is
  required when the atomic is a latch guarding other memory, because it
  establishes the happens-before edge; SeqCst is the simple, slower choice.
  Atomics also beat locks for in-flight counters on the hot path, and they
  compose with bounded-concurrency patterns. The practical trap is false
  sharing: two hot counters in one cache line contend even though nothing is
  shared logically.
when_to_use:
  - counters, gauges, flags, shutdown latches, one-word state
avoid_when:
  - several related fields (you would need a lock or a single versioned word)
minimal_example: |
  use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};

  static REQUESTS: AtomicU64 = AtomicU64::new(0);
  REQUESTS.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
  let n = REQUESTS.load(Ordering::Relaxed);
  let _ = n;
failure_modes:
  - Relaxed where the atomic guards other data, so the data is published too late
  - assuming atomics are free when they are a contended cache line
related:
  - rust.mutex
  - rust.arc
  - design.observability
sources:
  - https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/atomic/
